.\"
.Dd $Mdocdate: November 25 2026 $
.Dt SLCTL 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm slctl
.Nd control the Starlink dish monitor
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm slctl
.Op Fl 0
.Cm init Ns | Ns Cm passwd Ns | Ns Cm status Ns | Ns Cm top Ns | Ns Cm once
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Op Fl d Ar db
.Op Fl l Ar host:port
.Nm slctl
.Op Fl 0
.Cm top
.Op Ar seconds
.Op Fl c Ar file
.Op Fl d Ar db
.Op Fl l Ar host:port
.Sh DESCRIPTION
.Nm
is the operator tool for
.Xr sld 8 .
It does not fork a daemon.
.Bl -tag -width status
.It Cm init
Create the database and store the admin user and password.
Refuses uid 0 with the line
.Bd -literal -offset indent
slctl: doas -u _sl slctl init
.Ed
.Pp
Run as
.Sy _sl ,
it prompts:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
Admin user [admin]:
Password:
Repeat password:
.Ed
.Pp
An empty user name stores
.Li admin .
The password is read with echo off, must be at least 8 characters,
and must be entered the same way twice.
It is stored with
.Xr crypt 3
bcrypt
.Pq Li $2b$ .
A 32-byte session key is read from
.Pa /dev/urandom .
.Nm sld
does not rotate that key while it is running.
A second
.Cm init
prints that the database is already initialized and does not change
the password.
The prompt needs a terminal.
.It Cm passwd
Replace the admin user, the password, and the session key on a
database that
.Cm init
has already created.
Refuses uid 0 with the line
.Bd -literal -offset indent
slctl: doas -u _sl slctl passwd
.Ed
.Pp
The prompts and the password rules are the same as
.Cm init .
An empty user name stores
.Li admin .
On success it prints
.Bd -literal -offset indent
slctl: admin user reset
slctl: rcctl restart sld
.Ed
.Pp
Existing login cookies stay valid until
.Nm sld
is restarted, because the running process still holds the previous
session key.
The command writes those settings and does not roll up stored samples.
A database that is not initialized is left untouched, and the command
prints the same
.Cm init
hint
.Nm sld
prints.
.It Cm status
Print whether the database is initialized, which driver is configured,
the database path, the dish host, the HTTP port, and how many history
samples are stored.
This opens the database read-only.
The database is mode 0600 and owned by
.Sy _sl ,
so this is run as
.Sy _sl
or as root.
.It Cm top
On a terminal, draw the monitor and refresh it until
.Ic q
or
.Dv SIGINT .
The default interval is 3 seconds.
.Ar seconds
selects another interval.
The floor is 1 second.
.Fl 0 ,
written before the command, prints one sample and exits.
A standard output that is not a terminal does the same, so a pipe
does not spin.
That sample is the time, downlink and uplink bits per second,
latency, loss, obstruction fraction, dish uptime, and the open events.
An open event is a disconnect that has not come back, or a dish
outage whose recorded end is still at or after the current time.
Charts are ASCII and need at least 80 columns.
They grow with the height of the terminal.
A line under each chart marks even times across the window.
The dish map grows and shrinks with the terminal.
This opens the database read-only, does not open the dish, and does
not print the device id.
.Pp
The keys are:
.Bd -literal -offset indent
o   overview
l   quality
t   throughput
d   dish
h   history
c   config
H   24h
W   7d
M   30d
Y   365d
E   ever
+   one second longer
-   one second shorter
q   quit
.Ed
.It Cm once
Probe the dish once and print one status document as JSON.
This does not start
.Xr sld 8
and does not write the database.
If the dish port does not answer, the refusal is printed on standard
error and the exit status is still 0.
.El
.Pp
The options are as follows:
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Fl 0
Print one sample and exit.
Only recognized before the command, and only with
.Cm top .
.It Fl c Ar file
Configuration file.
.It Fl d Ar db
SQLite file for this command.
Overrides
.Va db_path .
.It Fl l Ar host:port
Listen address recorded for this command.
.Cm once
does not serve HTTP.
.El
.Sh FILES
.Bl -tag -width Ds
.It Pa /etc/sl.conf
Default configuration when
.Fl c
is passed by the operator.
See
.Xr sl.conf 5 .
.It Pa /var/sl/sl.db
Database created by
.Cm init
when
.Fl d
is not used.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Cm init
exits non-zero when run as root, when the prompt cannot be read,
or when the database directory is missing.
.Cm passwd
exits non-zero in those cases and when the database is not
initialized.
.Cm status
and
.Cm top
exit non-zero when the database cannot be opened.
.Cm once
exits 0 after printing status JSON, and also exits 0 when the dish
port does not answer.
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr crypt 3 ,
.Xr sl.conf 5 ,
.Xr sld 8
